<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  hexapod.sensors.xacro : IMU, CAMERA AND LIDAR
  ==========================================================================
  CHECKPOINT 6. Three sensors, each behind its own flag.

  WHAT A "SENSOR" IS IN A URDF, AND WHY IT IS TWO SEPARATE THINGS
  ==========================================================================
  A simulated sensor is always two declarations that must agree:

    1. A LINK, in the URDF proper. This is geometry and a coordinate frame.
       It says where the device physically sits and how heavy it is. RViz,
       TF and the physics engine all use this and none of them know it is a
       sensor at all.

    2. A <gazebo><sensor> block. This is the simulated PHYSICS of measuring:
       the ray casting, the image rendering, the noise model, and the plugin
       that turns the result into a ROS message.

  The link exists whether or not you are simulating. The sensor block exists
  only in simulation, because on real hardware the driver publishes the same
  topic and nothing else in the stack can tell the difference. That is the
  same swappable boundary as the hardware interface, applied to perception,
  and it is why these blocks are gated on the sim flag at the include site
  rather than written into the link definitions.

  WHY EACH SENSOR IS OPTIONAL, AND WHY TWO DEFAULT TO OFF
  ==========================================================================
  Rendering is the single most expensive thing Gazebo Classic does. A 640x480
  camera at 15 Hz costs far more than the entire 18 joint physics solve, and
  a 360 sample lidar at 10 Hz is 3600 ray casts per second through the whole
  world geometry. Turning both on can halve the real time factor, which
  matters because the gait is tuned against simulated time: a robot that
  walks correctly at RTF 1.0 can look quite different at RTF 0.4.

  So the defaults are chosen to CHANGE NOTHING that already works. The IMU is
  on, because it renders nothing and costs almost zero. The camera and lidar
  are off until you ask for them:

      ros2 launch hexapod_bringup hexapod_sim.launch.py \
           enable_camera:=true enable_lidar:=true

  Every launch you have run so far behaves exactly as before.

  MASS IS NOT FREE EITHER
  ==========================================================================
  These are real numbers for real parts, not placeholders: an RPLIDAR A1 is
  about 170 g, a Pi camera module with its mount about 50 g, a breakout IMU
  about 20 g. With everything enabled that is 0.24 kg on a 2.99 kg robot,
  roughly 8 percent, and it sits ABOVE and FORWARD of the centre of mass. It
  therefore raises the stance torque slightly and moves the centre of mass
  forward, which shrinks the stability margin of the rear support triangle.
  Small, but real, and worth one sentence in the report rather than being
  discovered as an unexplained change in behaviour.

  Every fixed joint is collapsed into its parent by Gazebo at load time, so
  these masses end up added to base_link exactly as the real parts would be
  bolted to the real chassis.
==========================================================================  -->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <xacro:arg name="enable_imu"    default="true"/>
  <xacro:arg name="enable_camera" default="false"/>
  <xacro:arg name="enable_lidar"  default="false"/>

  <xacro:property name="use_imu"    value="$(arg enable_imu)"/>
  <xacro:property name="use_camera" value="$(arg enable_camera)"/>
  <xacro:property name="use_lidar"  value="$(arg enable_lidar)"/>

  <!-- Mount points, derived from the body box rather than typed in, so that
       changing body_length or body_height moves the sensors with it. The
       front face is at +body_length/2 and the top face at +body_height/2. -->
  <xacro:property name="cam_x"   value="${body_length/2}"/>
  <xacro:property name="cam_z"   value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="cam_pitch" value="${25.0 * pi / 180.0}"/>
  <xacro:property name="lidar_z" value="${body_height/2 + 0.030}"/>

  <material name="sensor_black">
    <color rgba="0.08 0.08 0.10 1.0"/>
  </material>

  <!-- ====================================================================
       IMU

       WHAT IT IS. An inertial measurement unit reports angular velocity from
       three gyroscopes and linear acceleration from three accelerometers. It
       measures nothing about the world, only about its own motion, so it
       drifts without bound if you integrate it and is useless on its own for
       position. What it IS good for is attitude: gravity gives the
       accelerometers a permanent downward reference, so roll and pitch are
       observable and bounded. Yaw is not, which is why a magnetometer or a
       lidar is needed to fix heading.

       WHY A WALKING ROBOT NEEDS ONE. Everything downstream assumes the body
       is roughly level. The gait places feet in body coordinates, so if the
       body pitches 10 degrees the whole support polygon tilts with it and
       the commanded stance height is no longer the true height above ground.
       A wheeled robot can ignore this. A legged robot cannot, because it
       generates its own attitude disturbance every single step.

       WHY IT MOUNTS AT THE CENTRE OF MASS. An accelerometer at radius r from
       the rotation centre also measures centripetal and tangential terms,
       w x (w x r) and alpha x r, which are indistinguishable from real
       acceleration. Mounting at the centre of mass makes r zero and those
       terms vanish. On a body that rocks every step this is the difference
       between a usable signal and one that needs the lever arm subtracted
       out in software.

       BEGINNER MISTAKE. Trusting the orientation field. Gazebo's IMU plugin
       can report ground truth orientation, which looks wonderful and teaches
       you nothing, because the real device has no such output. Setting
       initial_orientation_as_reference false keeps it honest: orientation is
       integrated from the gyro like the real thing, drift included.
  ==================================================================== -->
  <xacro:if value="${use_imu}">
    <link name="imu_link">
      <visual>
        <geometry><box size="0.020 0.020 0.005"/></geometry>
        <material name="sensor_black"/>
      </visual>
      <xacro:inertial_box mass="0.020" x="0.020" y="0.020" z="0.005">
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      </xacro:inertial_box>
    </link>

    <joint name="imu_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="base_link"/>
      <child  link="imu_link"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>

    <gazebo reference="imu_link">
      <sensor name="imu_sensor" type="imu">
        <always_on>true</always_on>
        <update_rate>100</update_rate>
        <visualize>false</visualize>
        <!-- Noise densities are representative of an MPU6050 class part.
             Zero noise is the wrong default: it lets a filter be tuned to
             perfection in simulation and then fall apart on hardware. -->
        <imu>
          <angular_velocity>
            <x><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0.0</mean><stddev>2e-4</stddev></noise></x>
            <y><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0.0</mean><stddev>2e-4</stddev></noise></y>
            <z><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0.0</mean><stddev>2e-4</stddev></noise></z>
          </angular_velocity>
          <linear_acceleration>
            <x><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0.0</mean><stddev>1.7e-2</stddev></noise></x>
            <y><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0.0</mean><stddev>1.7e-2</stddev></noise></y>
            <z><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0.0</mean><stddev>1.7e-2</stddev></noise></z>
          </linear_acceleration>
        </imu>
        <plugin name="imu_plugin" filename="libgazebo_ros_imu_sensor.so">
          <ros>
            <namespace>/</namespace>
            <remapping>~/out:=imu/data</remapping>
          </ros>
          <initial_orientation_as_reference>false</initial_orientation_as_reference>
          <frame_name>imu_link</frame_name>
        </plugin>
      </sensor>
    </gazebo>
  </xacro:if>

  <!-- ====================================================================
       FORWARD CAMERA

       PURPOSE. This is the sensor checkpoint 8 detects stems with, so its
       aim is chosen by what it must see, not by where it looks tidy.

       THE GEOMETRY, WORKED OUT RATHER THAN GUESSED. The body stands about
       0.125 m above the ground. The camera is pitched down 25 degrees and
       has a 60 degree horizontal field of view, which at 4:3 is about 46
       degrees vertically, so rays leave between 2 and 48 degrees below
       horizontal. Where they meet the ground:

           near edge   0.125 / tan(48 deg) = 0.11 m ahead
           far edge    0.125 / tan(2 deg)  = 3.6 m ahead

       So a stem stays in frame from 3.6 m away right down to 11 cm, which is
       inside the reach of a nozzle on the front of the body. That continuity
       is the whole requirement: a controller that loses its target during
       the final approach has to guess, and guessing is what makes a demo
       fail in front of an examiner. Pitch it further down and the robot goes
       blind at range; level it out and it loses the stem just as it arrives.

       TWO FRAMES, AND WHY THE SECOND ONE IS NOT OPTIONAL. ROS has two
       conventions that disagree on purpose. REP 103 says a body frame is X
       forward, Y left, Z up. REP 145 says an optical frame is Z forward,
       X right, Y down, because that is what every camera calibration and
       projection formula in computer vision assumes. camera_link follows the
       first, camera_optical_link the second, and the fixed joint between
       them carries the rotation that converts one to the other.

       The plugin publishes with frame_name set to the OPTICAL frame. Point
       it at camera_link instead and nothing errors: image_proc, any
       projection from pixel to ray, and RViz's camera display are all simply
       rotated 90 degrees, and you chase that as a vision bug for an evening.
  ==================================================================== -->
  <xacro:if value="${use_camera}">
    <link name="camera_link">
      <visual>
        <geometry><box size="0.015 0.030 0.030"/></geometry>
        <material name="sensor_black"/>
      </visual>
      <xacro:inertial_box mass="0.050" x="0.015" y="0.030" z="0.030">
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      </xacro:inertial_box>
    </link>

    <joint name="camera_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="base_link"/>
      <child  link="camera_link"/>
      <origin xyz="${cam_x} 0 ${cam_z}" rpy="0 ${cam_pitch} 0"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- The REP 103 to REP 145 rotation. Read as: roll minus 90, then yaw
         minus 90. Nothing about the physical device changes; this is purely
         a relabelling of axes for the benefit of vision code. -->
    <link name="camera_optical_link"/>
    <joint name="camera_optical_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="camera_link"/>
      <child  link="camera_optical_link"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${-pi/2} 0 ${-pi/2}"/>
    </joint>

    <gazebo reference="camera_link">
      <material>Gazebo/Black</material>
      <sensor name="front_camera" type="camera">
        <always_on>true</always_on>
        <!-- 15 Hz, not 30. Detection runs far slower than this anyway, and
             every frame is a full render of the world. -->
        <update_rate>15.0</update_rate>
        <visualize>false</visualize>
        <camera name="front">
          <horizontal_fov>1.0472</horizontal_fov>
          <image>
            <width>640</width>
            <height>480</height>
            <format>R8G8B8</format>
          </image>
          <clip>
            <near>0.02</near>
            <far>30.0</far>
          </clip>
          <noise>
            <type>gaussian</type>
            <mean>0.0</mean>
            <stddev>0.007</stddev>
          </noise>
        </camera>
        <plugin name="camera_controller" filename="libgazebo_ros_camera.so">
          <ros>
            <namespace>/</namespace>
          </ros>
          <camera_name>camera</camera_name>
          <frame_name>camera_optical_link</frame_name>
          <hack_baseline>0.0</hack_baseline>
        </plugin>
      </sensor>
    </gazebo>
  </xacro:if>

  <!-- ====================================================================
       LIDAR

       WHAT IT IS. A spinning rangefinder that reports distance to the
       nearest obstacle at a few hundred angles around a horizontal circle.
       Unlike a camera it measures geometry directly, in metres, and does not
       care about lighting or colour. That is exactly why mapping uses it: a
       scan can be matched against a map without any appearance model.

       WHY IT IS MOUNTED HIGH. Two reasons, both learned the hard way by
       everyone who mounts one low. First, self occlusion: a scan plane that
       passes through the robot sees the robot, and those returns look like a
       wall wrapped around the sensor. Mounting 30 mm above the top face puts
       the plane clear of the chassis. Second, LEG occlusion, which is the
       hexapod specific version: six legs swinging through a scan plane at
       body height would chop it into moving shadows, and no scan matcher can
       tell a leg from a table leg.

       THE PROBLEM A LEGGED ROBOT HAS AND A WHEELED ONE DOES NOT. Scan
       matching assumes successive scans come from the same plane. A hexapod
       pitches and rolls a degree or two every step, so the plane tilts, and
       at 5 m a 2 degree tilt moves the apparent surface by 17 cm. That is
       larger than most map resolutions, and it is why checkpoint 7 is scoped
       to producing a map rather than navigating from one. Honest scoping is
       worth more in a report than a map that quietly does not close.

       min_range MUST clear the robot. 0.12 m is beyond the widest point of
       the chassis, so the sensor cannot return its own frame.
  ==================================================================== -->
  <xacro:if value="${use_lidar}">
    <link name="lidar_link">
      <visual>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="0.035" length="0.040"/></geometry>
        <material name="sensor_black"/>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="0.035" length="0.040"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <inertial>
        <mass value="0.170"/>
        <!-- Solid cylinder about Z: Ixx = Iyy = m(3r^2 + h^2)/12,
             Izz = m r^2 / 2. Written out rather than reusing
             inertial_cylinder_x, whose axis of symmetry is X. -->
        <inertia ixx="6.47e-5" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0"
                 iyy="6.47e-5" iyz="0.0"
                 izz="1.04e-4"/>
      </inertial>
    </link>

    <joint name="lidar_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="base_link"/>
      <child  link="lidar_link"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 ${lidar_z}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>

    <gazebo reference="lidar_link">
      <material>Gazebo/Black</material>
      <sensor name="lidar" type="ray">
        <always_on>true</always_on>
        <update_rate>10</update_rate>
        <!-- Set true to see the blue fan in the Gazebo window. Costs frame
             rate, so it is a debugging aid and not a default. -->
        <visualize>false</visualize>
        <ray>
          <scan>
            <horizontal>
              <samples>360</samples>
              <resolution>1</resolution>
              <min_angle>-3.14159</min_angle>
              <max_angle>3.14159</max_angle>
            </horizontal>
          </scan>
          <range>
            <min>0.12</min>
            <max>12.0</max>
            <resolution>0.015</resolution>
          </range>
          <noise>
            <type>gaussian</type>
            <mean>0.0</mean>
            <stddev>0.01</stddev>
          </noise>
        </ray>
        <plugin name="lidar_controller" filename="libgazebo_ros_ray_sensor.so">
          <ros>
            <namespace>/</namespace>
            <remapping>~/out:=scan</remapping>
          </ros>
          <output_type>sensor_msgs/LaserScan</output_type>
          <frame_name>lidar_link</frame_name>
        </plugin>
      </sensor>
    </gazebo>
  </xacro:if>

</robot>
